Context
  Asked whether anything here could wrap paredit or an Emacs formatter for Guile
  source the way the plugin docs' scripts/format.sh hook example does; then for a
  consistent Emacs setup across every tool even when wrapped in shell; then for the
  JSON files to carry a known contract version so a contract change throws; then --
  crucially -- that the specs for configuration must be ISOLATED from the in-practice
  JSON that drives the config, to protect future agents.

  That last constraint arrived before I had put a version marker in plugin.json's
  metadata, which is what I had been about to do. contracts/ exists because of it.

Deviations
  The hook checks rather than formats. The request pointed at a formatter example;
  the anti-pattern in the record is broken delimiters, not inconsistent indentation,
  and a hook that rewrites files after every edit is a surprise this repository's
  own conventions argue against. Formatting is a separate, opt-in target.

Considerations
  `gmake fmt' over the whole tree is not run here and should not be run casually --
  it would reindent four existing files and produce a diff unrelated to any change.
  check-scheme is the safe half and is the one in `checks'.

  The contract pin is a tripwire, not a guarantee: it fails when the CLI version
  moves, which is the only drift signal available without re-running every planted
  fault. The fault lists are in contracts/artifacts.json precisely so a re-measure
  is mechanical rather than remembered.

  hooks/ validation being partial while monitors/ has none is worth re-testing on a
  newer CLI. Both claims are versioned in contracts/cell.json for that reason.

Timeline
  2026-09-29, nexus, FreeBSD 15.1-RELEASE, guile3 3.0.10, GNU Emacs 30.2, Claude Code
  2.1.261, as dsp-dr. gmake checks green with check-contracts and check-scheme added;
  validate --strict passing; four contract faults and four hook cases calibrated.
